{
  "ResearchDossier": {
    "topic": "where the @ sign in email addresses comes from",
    "summary": "The @ sign was a merchant's unit for an amphora of wine in a 1536 letter, became accounting shorthand for 'at the rate of', reached typewriters by 1900, and was chosen by Ray Tomlinson in 1971 to split user from host in the first networked email because nobody used it in names.",
    "angle": "The symbol in your email address is a 500-year-old wine measurement.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "id": "c1",
        "text": "Historian Giorgio Stabile traced the @ symbol to a mercantile letter sent by Florentine Francesco Lapi from Seville to Rome on 4 May 1536, where it meant an amphora (anfora), a unit of weight and volume; the document concerns the price of an @ of wine in Peru.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/At_sign",
            "title": "At sign - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "the price of an @ of wine in Peru"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c2",
        "text": "The @ is used as an accounting and invoice abbreviation meaning 'at a rate of'.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/At_sign",
            "title": "At sign - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "used as an accounting and invoice abbreviation meaning \"at a rate of\""
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c3",
        "text": "The @ appeared on Underwood typewriters from the Underwood No. 5 in 1900 onward.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/At_sign",
            "title": "At sign - Wikipedia"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "medium"
      },
      {
        "id": "c4",
        "text": "In 1971 Ray Tomlinson created the first networked email system; the @ was chosen because it was not used in usernames or in TENEX programming, separating the user name from the host.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ray_Tomlinson",
            "title": "Ray Tomlinson - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "The symbol was chosen because it was not used in usernames or in TENEX programming."
          },
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/At_sign",
            "title": "At sign - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "Ray Tomlinson of BBN Technologies is credited for having introduced this usage in 1971."
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c5",
        "text": "Tomlinson's first email was a test message between two computers placed side by side.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ray_Tomlinson",
            "title": "Ray Tomlinson - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "a test message between two computers placed side by side"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c6",
        "text": "In 2010 the Museum of Modern Art added the @ symbol to its design collection.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/At_sign",
            "title": "At sign - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "In 2010, the Museum of Modern Art (MoMA) added the @ symbol to its design collection."
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      }
    ],
